export function language(add,cpp){
add('control-flow','branch-contracts','Branch on a domain condition',
'A Boolean condition should answer a domain question: is this score passing, is this command recognized, or is this collection empty? Put the rule in one place so boundary cases can be named and tested.\n\nAn if/else chain selects the first matching branch. Overlapping conditions therefore make order part of the contract.',
cpp('iostream',"    for (int score : {59, 60, 100}) {\n        if (score >= 60)\n            std::cout << \"pass\\n\";\n        else\n            std::cout << \"retry\\n\";\n    }"),'retry\npass\npass',
'The boundary is inclusive: 59 fails, while 60 and 100 pass. The loop supplies three independent inputs to the same decision.',
'Testing only a value far from the boundary can miss an incorrect > in place of >=.',
['Which score first passes this rule?','60','59','61'],'The comparison includes equality at 60.',
'Add 0 and 101. Decide whether invalid scores need a separate rejection branch before the pass/fail decision.');
add('control-flow','short-circuit','Use short-circuiting to establish a precondition',
'Built-in && evaluates its left operand first and evaluates the right operand only when the left is true. This lets a guard establish a precondition for a later expression.\n\nThe order is a language guarantee for built-in logical operators. Keep the expression readable; complicated side effects make a compact guard harder to audit.',
cpp('iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> values;\n    bool starts_with_seven = !values.empty() && values.front() == 7;\n    std::cout << starts_with_seven << \"\\n\";\n    values.push_back(7);\n    std::cout << (!values.empty() && values.front() == 7) << \"\\n\";"),'0\n1',
'The first check never calls front because the vector is empty. The second check reaches front after a valid element exists.',
'Bitwise & does not provide the same short-circuit guarantee as &&.',
['Why is front safe in this condition?','The empty check is evaluated first and gates the call','front returns zero for an empty vector','Both operands are always evaluated'],'The right operand runs only after the guard succeeds.',
'Reverse the operands on paper and explain the failed precondition. Add a helper that counts how often the right operand runs.');
add('control-flow','loop-invariants','State what a loop has already processed',
'A loop invariant describes the state at a chosen point in each iteration. For a prefix sum, the accumulator contains exactly the elements before the current index. Initialization, preservation, and termination connect that statement to the final result.\n\nKeep the arithmetic domain bounded while studying the proof; a mathematically correct recurrence does not excuse overflowing the accumulator.',
cpp('array iostream',"    const std::array<int, 3> values{3, 1, 4};\n    int sum = 0;\n    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < values.size(); ++i) {\n        std::cout << i << \":\" << sum << \"\\n\";\n        sum += values[i];\n    }\n    std::cout << \"total=\" << sum << \"\\n\";"),'0:0\n1:3\n2:4\ntotal=8',
'Each trace line is printed before adding values[i]. At termination the processed prefix is the complete array.',
'A final expected total is a postcondition; it does not by itself explain why each iteration preserves correctness.',
['Before iteration i, which elements are included?','Exactly the prefix [0,i)','All elements including i','Only the last element'],'The current element is added after the trace.',
'Change the array to include a negative value. Write the invariant independently of the actual numbers, then check the trace.');
add('control-flow','switch-enums','Dispatch a closed set of commands',
'An enum class names a closed set of alternatives without implicitly converting every enumerator to an integer. A switch can expose the behavior for each recognized command.\n\nKeep parsing separate from dispatch: this example starts with already classified commands. Unknown external text needs its own parser result rather than an invented enum value.',
cpp('iostream',"    for (Command command : {Command::list, Command::save}) {\n        switch (command) {\n        case Command::list:\n            std::cout << \"scan rows\\n\";\n            break;\n        case Command::save:\n            std::cout << \"publish snapshot\\n\";\n            break;\n        }\n    }","enum class Command { list, save };"),'scan rows\npublish snapshot',
'Each enumerator reaches one case, and break prevents execution from continuing into the following case.',
'Accidental fallthrough can perform two state changes for one command. Use an explicit annotation only when fallthrough is intended.',
['What prevents the list case from also saving?','break','The enum automatically inserts a return','The order of enum declarations'],'break exits the switch.',
'Add a count command and handle it in the switch. Compile with warnings enabled and inspect what happens when its case is missing.');
add('control-flow','termination','Make a decreasing measure visible',
'A progress measure should move toward a bound on every repetition. Repeatedly halving a positive integer is a simple example: the value decreases until it reaches zero.\n\nUnsigned arithmetic is defined modulo its range, so a loop condition such as i >= 0 cannot terminate an unsigned countdown. Choose a condition that reflects the remaining work.',
cpp('iostream',"    unsigned remaining = 13, steps = 0;\n    while (remaining != 0) {\n        std::cout << remaining << \" \";\n        remaining /= 2;\n        ++steps;\n    }\n    std::cout << \"steps=\" << steps << \"\\n\";"),'13 6 3 1 steps=4',
'The sequence reaches zero after four divisions. The counter measures iterations rather than elapsed time.',
'An unsigned countdown that decrements zero wraps to a large value instead of becoming negative.',
['Which quantity proves this loop progresses?','The positive remaining value decreases','The output stream gets larger','Unsigned values eventually become negative'],'Integer halving strictly reduces every positive value.',
'Try 0, 1, and a power of two. Predict the step count and document how the empty-work case behaves.');

add('functions-build','value-parameters','A value parameter gives independent state',
'Passing by value constructs a function-local value from the argument. Changes to that parameter do not change the caller’s object. This is often the simplest ownership contract for a small value or for an operation that needs its own mutable copy.\n\nA copy can have a real cost for a large collection. Choose the ownership behavior first, then consider whether moving or borrowing better fits the interface.',
cpp('iostream string',"    std::string original = \"Ada\";\n    std::cout << decorate(original) << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << original << \"\\n\";","std::string decorate(std::string value) {\n    value += \"!\";\n    return value;\n}"),'Ada!\nAda',
'decorate changes its local string and returns an owning result. The caller’s original string remains Ada.',
'Changing a parameter to a non-const reference changes the public mutation contract, even if the function name stays the same.',
['Why is original unchanged?','The parameter is a separate string value','All function arguments are const','Returning undoes mutation'],'The caller and parameter own distinct string objects.',
'Compare value, const-reference, and mutable-reference signatures. State which versions can implement this operation without changing the caller.');
add('functions-build','return-results','Return related results together',
'A function with several related outputs can return a small value type. This avoids output parameters whose initialization and failure behavior are unclear. Structured bindings give names to the returned components.\n\nThe result should express meaning rather than merely position. Named members also help callers that do not use structured bindings.',
cpp('iostream vector',"    auto [minimum, maximum] = bounds({4, 2, 9});\n    std::cout << minimum << \" \" << maximum << \"\\n\";","struct Bounds {\n    int minimum;\n    int maximum;\n};\nBounds bounds(const std::vector<int> &values) {\n    int low = values.at(0), high = low;\n    for (int value : values) {\n        if (value < low)\n            low = value;\n        if (value > high)\n            high = value;\n    }\n    return {low, high};\n}"),'2 9',
'Both fields are computed before the result is returned. This version requires nonempty input and at(0) reports a violation by throwing.',
'A function that needs a nonempty range must document that contract or represent the empty outcome explicitly.',
['How should an empty input be represented in a nonthrowing redesign?','An optional Bounds result','Two uninitialized integers','A reference to a local Bounds'],'optional can distinguish absence from valid minimum and maximum values.',
'Change the return type to optional<Bounds> and test empty, single-element, and repeated-element inputs.');
add('functions-build','overload-resolution','Overloads select from the static argument types',
'An overload set provides several functions with one name and different parameter lists. Resolution chooses a viable best match from the argument types and conversions at the call site.\n\nDo not use overloads whose meaning changes unexpectedly after an innocent numeric conversion. Small examples can make an interface’s selection rules observable.',
cpp('iostream','    show(7);\n    show(7.5);',"void show(int) {\n    std::cout << \"integer\\n\";\n}\nvoid show(double) {\n    std::cout << \"floating\\n\";\n}"),'integer\nfloating',
'The integer literal selects the int overload. The floating literal has type double and selects the double overload.',
'The value stored in a double does not make it an int for overload resolution, even when it is mathematically integral.',
['Which overload receives 7.0?','The double overload','The int overload','Whichever function is declared first'],'The literal’s static type is double.',
'Call show with a float and with a short. Explain the promotion that makes each overload a better match.');
add('functions-build','declarations-linking','A declaration states an interface before its definition',
'A declaration lets a caller use a function before the implementation appears. In a multi-file program, declarations normally live in headers and definitions live in source files. Each source file is compiled into a translation unit before the linker resolves external symbols.\n\nThe browser editor uses one source file, but declaration and definition rules still apply.',
cpp('iostream',"    std::cout << square(6) << \"\\n\";","int square(int value);\nint square(int value) {\n    return value * value;\n}"),'36',
'The declaration and definition agree on the parameter and return types. The caller sees one function, not two overloads.',
'Declaring a function without providing a needed definition can compile successfully and then fail at link time.',
['Which stage resolves a separately compiled function definition?','Linking','Lexing','The first call at runtime'],'The linker connects references to external definitions.',
'Move the declaration to a header and the definition to a second native source file. Build both files, then omit the implementation and inspect the link error.');
add('functions-build','header-guards','Headers must tolerate repeated inclusion',
'A header can be reached through several include paths in one translation unit. An include guard prevents its declarations from being processed repeatedly. Functions defined in headers also need appropriate linkage, commonly inline, to avoid duplicate definitions across translation units.\n\nThe inline keyword permits certain repeated definitions; it does not promise that the optimizer substitutes every call.',
cpp('iostream',"    std::cout << clamp_score(120) << \"\\n\";","#ifndef CPP_FOUNDRY_SCORE_HPP\n#define CPP_FOUNDRY_SCORE_HPP\ninline int clamp_score(int value) {\n    return value < 0 ? 0 : value > 100 ? 100 : value;\n}\n#endif"),'100',
'The guarded block behaves like a small header. clamp_score has the same contract in every translation unit that includes it.',
'A guard prevents repetition inside one translation unit; it does not solve every cross-file definition problem.',
['Does inline guarantee machine-code inlining?','No','Yes, for all calls','Only when a header guard exists'],'inline has language linkage/definition rules independent of optimizer decisions.',
'Create two source files including this header. Link them together and explain why the inline definition is permitted.');

add('text-sequences','string-bytes','Text length and byte length are different contracts',
'std::string owns a sequence of char units. With UTF-8 input, a character can occupy more than one byte, so size is a storage count rather than a user-perceived character count.\n\nThe record store limits UTF-8 bytes because the file format stores a byte length. Display width and Unicode segmentation require different algorithms.',
cpp('iostream string',"    std::string name = \"Zoë\";\n    std::cout << \"bytes=\" << name.size() << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << name << \"\\n\";"),'bytes=4\nZoë',
'Z and o occupy one byte each; ë occupies two UTF-8 bytes. The browser runtime preserves the byte sequence through output decoding.',
'Truncating at an arbitrary byte boundary can split a UTF-8 sequence and create invalid text.',
['What does size report for this UTF-8 string?','Four bytes','Three Unicode characters','Four screen columns'],'The string counts stored char units, not grapheme clusters.',
'Try a different multibyte name. Inspect its bytes and identify valid code-point boundaries before designing truncation.');
add('text-sequences','vector-capacity','Size describes live elements; capacity describes storage',
'A vector may own more storage than it currently uses for live elements. reserve requests capacity without constructing new elements. resize changes size and constructs or destroys elements as needed.\n\nAvoid asserting an exact growth factor: the standard specifies capacity guarantees and amortized behavior, not a universal doubling policy.',
cpp('iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> values{1, 2};\n    values.reserve(20);\n    std::cout << \"size=\" << values.size() << \" enough=\" << (values.capacity() >= 20)\n              << \"\\n\";\n    values.resize(4);\n    std::cout << values.at(2) << \" \" << values.at(3) << \"\\n\";"),'size=2 enough=1\n0 0',
'reserve leaves two live elements. resize constructs two additional int elements, value-initialized to zero.',
'Accessing an index below capacity but at or above size still violates the element-access precondition.',
['After reserve alone, is element 19 alive?','No','Yes, initialized to zero','Only when capacity is exactly 20'],'Capacity is storage availability, not the number of constructed elements.',
'Reserve a smaller amount after the first call and inspect capacity. Add a type with visible construction/destruction to study resize.');
add('text-sequences','line-input','Frame input as complete lines',
'A line-oriented command language needs clear framing before token parsing. getline extracts one line without retaining the delimiter, which prevents a malformed command from borrowing the next command’s text accidentally.\n\nThis example uses an in-memory stream for deterministic input. The same getline loop can read preloaded stdin in the browser or a native stream.',
cpp('iostream sstream string',"    std::istringstream input(\"LIST\\n\\nSAVE\\n\");\n    std::string line;\n    while (std::getline(input, line))\n        std::cout << \"[\" << line << \"]\\n\";"),'[LIST]\n[]\n[SAVE]',
'The empty line is a legitimate framed record. The application can choose to ignore it after framing, as the capstone does.',
'Mixing formatted extraction with getline can leave a newline waiting in the stream. Handle that transition deliberately.',
['Does getline keep the newline in line?','No','Yes','Only for empty lines'],'The delimiter is extracted but not appended to the string.',
'Add a final line without a newline and verify it is still processed. Decide how an overlong command should be rejected.');
add('text-sequences','erase-remove','Remove elements with an explicit predicate',
'C++20 provides erase_if for standard sequence containers. It removes elements satisfying a predicate and reports how many were removed. This avoids confusing a logical partition operation with a change in container size.\n\nReferences and iterators into a vector can be invalidated by erasure. Finish the mutation before establishing new observers.',
cpp('iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> values{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    auto removed = std::erase_if(values, [](int value) { return value % 2 == 0; });\n    std::cout << \"removed=\" << removed << \" remaining=\" << values.size() << \"\\n\";\n    for (int value : values)\n        std::cout << value;\n    std::cout << \"\\n\";"),'removed=2 remaining=3\n135',
'Two even values are erased. The surviving odd values retain their relative order in the vector.',
'Do not keep using an iterator to an erased or shifted element after vector erasure.',
['What changes after erase_if?','The vector size and element positions can change','Only the capacity changes','The original elements are all still present'],'erase_if performs actual removal, not just a search.',
'Test no matches, all matches, and an empty vector. Save an index before deletion and decide whether it still names the same record.');
add('text-sequences','safe-slicing','Build subranges from validated bounds',
'A slice needs both a start and a length that fit the source. Validate using subtraction after checking the start, rather than adding start+length where an unsigned sum could wrap.\n\nA string_view slice borrows the source. Returning an owning string instead is useful when the caller cannot maintain the source lifetime.',
cpp('iostream optional string_view',"    auto part = slice(\"Lovelace\", 0, 4);\n    if (part)\n        std::cout << *part << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"rejected=\" << !slice(\"Ada\", 2, 9) << \"\\n\";","std::optional<std::string_view> slice(std::string_view text, std::size_t start,\n                                      std::size_t length) {\n    if (start > text.size() || length > text.size() - start)\n        return std::nullopt;\n    return text.substr(start, length);\n}"),'Love\nrejected=1',
'The first request selects four bytes. The second asks for more bytes than remain after position two and is rejected before slicing.',
'A valid byte slice of UTF-8 is not necessarily a valid slice at Unicode boundaries.',
['Why subtract only after checking start?','To avoid underflow in size-start','To allocate a new buffer','Because substr always copies'],'The guard establishes that the unsigned subtraction is representable.',
'Test an empty slice at the end, an out-of-range start, and a very large length. Document byte versus character semantics.');

add('user-types','aggregate-init','Aggregate initialization names a simple value layout',
'An aggregate can collect fields without a custom constructor. Brace initialization makes the initial state visible and catches many narrowing conversions. Default member initializers provide a deliberate value for omitted members.\n\nA public aggregate is suitable when callers may freely construct valid combinations. Use controlled construction when fields must satisfy a stronger invariant.',
cpp('iostream string',"    Person first{1, \"Ada\", 90};\n    Person second{2, \"Grace\"};\n    std::cout << first.name << \" \" << first.score << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << second.name << \" \" << second.score << \"\\n\";","struct Person {\n    unsigned id;\n    std::string name;\n    unsigned score = 0;\n};"),'Ada 90\nGrace 0',
'The first value supplies all fields. The omitted score in the second uses its declared default initializer.',
'A public aggregate cannot prevent a caller from supplying an invalid ID or score by itself.',
['Where does second.score get zero?','Its default member initializer','All omitted fields are always uninitialized','The string constructor writes it'],'The field declaration explicitly supplies its default.',
'Add an ID validation requirement and decide whether this aggregate should become a class with controlled construction.');
add('user-types','enum-status','Return a named status instead of a magic integer',
'A named enum makes result alternatives explicit: inserted, duplicate, and full have different meanings even if all are represented by small integers. enum class avoids accidental implicit conversion to an unrelated arithmetic value.\n\nKeep the externally visible behavior in the API contract rather than requiring callers to know the numeric representation of an enumerator.',
cpp('iostream',"    Status result = Status::duplicate;\n    std::cout << (result == Status::duplicate ? \"duplicate\" : \"other\") << \"\\n\";","enum class Status { inserted, duplicate, full };"),'duplicate',
'The caller compares a named alternative. Reordering the enumerators would not change this source-level decision.',
'Serializing an enum’s underlying bytes without a documented encoding ties the file to implementation details.',
['Should callers depend on duplicate being integer 1?','No, they should use its named contract','Yes, enum order is a persistence format','Only when printing'],'The name expresses the API alternative; storage encoding is a separate decision.',
'Add missing and invalid statuses. Write a formatter that handles each named outcome without exposing its integer value.');
add('user-types','explicit-construction','Prevent accidental domain conversions',
'A one-argument constructor can become an implicit conversion unless it is explicit. A domain ID type often benefits from requiring the caller to state that conversion at the boundary.\n\nThe wrapper can also establish invariants and keep unrelated numeric domains, such as page IDs and row IDs, distinct.',
cpp('iostream stdexcept',"    RowId id{7};\n    std::cout << id.value() << \"\\n\";","class RowId {\n    unsigned value_;\n\n  public:\n    explicit RowId(unsigned value) : value_(value) {\n        if (!value)\n            throw std::invalid_argument(\"zero ID\");\n    }\n    unsigned value() const {\n        return value_;\n    }\n};"),'7',
'Direct brace construction explicitly creates the domain type. A raw unsigned cannot silently stand in for RowId at an unrelated call site.',
'explicit does not validate the value automatically; the constructor still needs the domain check.',
['What does explicit control?','Implicit conversion through this constructor','Whether construction can throw','Whether the value is stored on the heap'],'The validation and allocation choices are separate from implicit conversion rules.',
'Write a function accepting RowId and attempt to pass 7 directly. Compare that diagnostic with passing RowId{7}.');
add('user-types','member-const','Const member functions promise read-only access through this',
'A const-qualified member function can be called on a const object and cannot modify ordinary members through its this pointer. It is useful for observation without exposing mutable storage.\n\nConst is an access-path rule, not a complete concurrency or deep-immutability guarantee. A returned mutable pointer could undermine the intended public contract.',
cpp('iostream',"    const Counter counter{3};\n    std::cout << counter.value() << \"\\n\";","class Counter {\n    int count_;\n\n  public:\n    explicit Counter(int count) : count_(count) {\n    }\n    int value() const {\n        return count_;\n    }\n};"),'3',
'value can observe the const counter because its member declaration is const-qualified.',
'Returning a mutable reference from a const observer can defeat the abstraction even if it is technically possible through other indirection.',
['Why is value callable on counter?','It is a const-qualified observer','All member functions accept const objects','The return type is int'],'The qualifier describes access through this during the call.',
'Add an increment member without const. Verify that it is rejected for a const Counter and accepted for a mutable one.');
add('user-types','value-equality','Define equality from domain meaning',
'C++20 can generate memberwise equality with a defaulted operator==. This is useful for a small value type whose equality really is equality of all its members.\n\nDo not confuse object address, key identity, and full value equality. A record can have the same key but a changed score; your API should specify which comparison each operation needs.',
cpp('iostream string',"    Row a{1, \"Ada\", 90}, b{1, \"Ada\", 90}, c{1, \"Ada\", 95};\n    std::cout << (a == b) << \" \" << (a == c) << \"\\n\";","struct Row {\n    unsigned id;\n    std::string name;\n    unsigned score;\n    bool operator==(const Row &) const = default;\n};"),'1 0',
'a and b match in every field. c has the same key but a different score, so full value equality is false.',
'Comparing only IDs when checking whether a persisted snapshot changed can miss changed payload fields.',
['Are a and c fully equal?','No, their scores differ','Yes, their IDs match','Only if their addresses match'],'Defaulted equality compares every declared member.',
'Add a separate same_key function and show a case where same_key is true while full equality is false.');

add('standard-library','iterator-ranges','Use half-open iterator ranges',
'Standard algorithms commonly use a half-open range [first,last): first names an element and last marks the position just past the range. An empty range has first==last and requires no special element sentinel.\n\nThe past-the-end iterator is a boundary marker and must not be dereferenced. It remains useful as the not-found result of search algorithms.',
cpp('algorithm iostream vector',"    std::vector<int> values{2, 4, 6};\n    auto found = std::find(values.begin(), values.end(), 4);\n    if (found != values.end())\n        std::cout << *found << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"absent=\"\n              << (std::find(values.begin(), values.end(), 9) == values.end()) << \"\\n\";"),'4\nabsent=1',
'The first search returns an element iterator; the second returns the boundary marker and is compared without dereferencing it.',
'Dereferencing end is invalid even when the container has allocated spare capacity.',
['What does end identify?','A past-the-end position','The last element','An element containing zero'],'The algorithm excludes that boundary from its inspected elements.',
'Search an empty vector and a one-element vector. Check the iterator against end before reading through it.');
add('standard-library','transform-output','Make the destination of a transformation explicit',
'A transformation applies a mapping to each input element. The output range must have valid writable positions or use an insertion iterator that grows the destination.\n\nWriting through begin on an empty vector does not create elements. back_inserter expresses the desired append behavior and delegates ownership to the output vector.',
cpp('algorithm iostream iterator vector',"    std::vector<int> input{1, 2, 3}, output;\n    std::transform(input.begin(), input.end(), std::back_inserter(output),\n                   [](int value) { return value * value; });\n    for (int value : output)\n        std::cout << value << \" \";\n    std::cout << \"size=\" << output.size() << \"\\n\";"),'1 4 9 size=3',
'Each mapped value is appended to output. Its final size equals the number of input elements.',
'reserve alone does not make output.begin dereferenceable when size is zero.',
['Why is back_inserter used?','It appends new elements','It sorts the input','It prevents all allocations'],'The insertion iterator calls push_back on the destination.',
'Implement a pre-sized output version and compare its size contract with the insertion version. Test empty input.');
add('standard-library','optional-observation','Distinguish absence from a present zero',
'optional represents either a live value or no value. A present numeric zero is still present, so the optional’s Boolean test describes engagement rather than the truth of the contained number.\n\nChoose defaults deliberately: value_or can simplify presentation, but using a default internally can erase an important distinction between missing and present data.',
cpp('iostream optional',"    std::optional<int> zero = 0, missing;\n    std::cout << \"present=\" << bool(zero) << \" value=\" << *zero << \"\\n\";\n    std::cout << \"missing=\" << !missing << \" fallback=\" << missing.value_or(-1) << \"\\n\";"),'present=1 value=0\nmissing=1 fallback=-1',
'The first optional contains a constructed int equal to zero. The second is disengaged and uses the explicitly chosen fallback.',
'Dereferencing a disengaged optional violates its precondition; a default is not implicitly installed by operator*.',
['Is optional<int>{0} engaged?','Yes','No','Only after value_or is called'],'Engagement is independent of the integer’s value.',
'Model a missing score and a real score of zero. Decide where presentation defaults may be introduced without changing the stored meaning.');
add('standard-library','container-selection','Choose ordering and lookup contracts deliberately',
'A set maintains unique ordered keys. A vector preserves a sequence and allows duplicates. The correct representation depends on observable behavior, not just the name of a convenient method.\n\nThis example constructs a set from a sequence to produce a deterministic unique-key view. The original sequence remains available when duplicates or input order matter.',
cpp('iostream set vector',"    std::vector<int> input{3, 1, 3, 2};\n    std::set<int> unique(input.begin(), input.end());\n    for (int value : unique)\n        std::cout << value;\n    std::cout << \" input=\" << input.size() << \" unique=\" << unique.size() << \"\\n\";"),'123 input=4 unique=3',
'The set contains three ordered keys. Converting to it discards duplicate multiplicity, which would be incorrect for a query that needs duplicate rows.',
'A representation change can change semantics even when all distinct values are still present.',
['What information is lost in the set?','Duplicate multiplicity and original order','All key values','The ability to search'],'A set exposes unique sorted keys rather than the original sequence.',
'Compare set, multiset, and vector on the same input. Specify whether your database operator must preserve duplicate rows.');
add('standard-library','algorithm-predicates','Predicates need coherent semantic rules',
'An algorithm’s predicate determines which values match or how values are ordered. For sorting, a comparator must satisfy strict weak ordering; inconsistent answers invalidate the algorithm’s assumptions.\n\nA projection can select a field while a standard comparator supplies the ordering. Keep a deterministic tie rule when observable output requires one.',
cpp('algorithm iostream string vector',"    std::vector<std::string> names{\"Grace\", \"Ada\", \"Linus\"};\n    std::ranges::sort(names, [](const auto &a, const auto &b) {\n        return a.size() == b.size() ? a < b : a.size() < b.size();\n    });\n    for (const auto &name : names)\n        std::cout << name << \"\\n\";"),'Ada\nGrace\nLinus',
'Ada is the only three-byte name. The length comparison places Ada first; the alphabetical tie-breaker orders Grace before Linus.',
'An unstable sort does not promise to preserve input order among equivalent elements. Use a tie-breaker or stable_sort when needed.',
['Does this comparator totally order equal-length names?','Yes, the comparator adds an alphabetical tie-breaker','No, it ignores equal-length names','Yes, sort adds a name comparison'],'The alphabetical tie-breaker distinguishes the equal-length names.',
'Add an alphabetical tie-breaker and test both original and reversed input. Explain why deterministic output needs a defined tie rule.');
}
